obo:BFO_0000066  a  owl:ObjectProperty .

gomodel:6ab067da00015739
        a                      owl:Ontology ;
        rdfs:comment           "Curated with GO AI HUB. Human counterpart of gomodel:6ab067da00015433: SLC16A1 and LDHB in the cardiomyocyte mitochondrial lactate oxidation route. Activity assertions are ISS from the mouse orthologs; IDA is used only for the localisation and cell context that PMID:42951956 shows for the human proteins." , "Curation notes: (1) LDHB 'enabled by' GO:0004459 is IDA PMID:27618187 (Nat Chem Biol 2016, mitochondrial lactate metabolism in human cells), NOT a transfer: the enzymatic activity of human LDHB is directly known, and human LDHB also holds this term by EXP PMID:11276087. (2) The ISS sources are the mouse experimental data of PMID:42951956 itself, captured in gomodel:6ab067da00015433; MGI does not yet carry standard annotations for mitochondrial MCT1 or matrix LDHB, so the mouse GO-CAM is the source of record. (3) MCT1 uses GO:0015129, not the child GO:0015650, because proton coupling at the inner mitochondrial membrane is not tested; human SLC16A1 holds GO:0015650 by IDA at the PLASMA membrane (PMID:12946269), a different activity. (4) Human LDHB's existing localisation annotation is mitochondrial inner membrane (IDA PMID:27618187); this model places it in the matrix, following the proteinase K data in PMID:42951956 Fig 3b. (5) Human SLC16A1 has no prior mitochondrial annotation; the inner-membrane localisation is new with PMID:42951956." , "EVIDENCE POLICY. PMID:42951956 establishes this mechanism in MOUSE. Its human experiments demonstrate LOCALISATION, not activity, so IDA is used only for localisation and cell context; every activity and transport assertion for the human proteins is ISS (ECO:0000250, GO_REF:0000024) with the mouse ortholog in with/from. IDA PMID:42951956: MCT1 'occurs in' mitochondrial inner membrane, and the cardiac muscle cell / heart context - from Fig 3a (MCT1 in mitochondrial fractions of human donor and failing hearts), Extended Data Fig 6a-d (MCT1 immunoprecipitated from human cardiac mitochondria, recovering mitochondrial and not plasma-membrane markers), Fig 3c (proteinase K protection on human hearts) and Extended Data Fig 2j (human snRNA-seq: SLC16A1 predominantly in cardiomyocytes). ISS with:MGI:MGI:106013 - MCT1 lactate transmembrane transporter activity, the cytosol-to-matrix lactate input and output, and the process. The paper does NOT demonstrate lactate transporter activity for human SLC16A1: the human functional experiment (Fig 3n) is a small-molecule MCT1-inhibitor treatment of purified human cardiac mitochondria, which shows the import is MCT1-inhibitor-sensitive but does not assay the human protein, so attributing the activity to SLC16A1 from it would rest on the inhibitor's selectivity rather than on direct evidence. ISS with:MGI:MGI:96763 - the entire mitochondrial context of LDHB (matrix localisation, lactate input, pyruvate and NADH outputs, process, cell context); the human panels do not attribute these to LDHB specifically, and the pan-LDH inhibitor experiment (Fig 3m) is mouse." , "Human counterpart of gomodel:6ab067da00015433 (Mouse). MCT1/SLC16A1 imports lactate across the inner mitochondrial membrane of cardiomyocytes; matrix LDHB oxidises it to pyruvate + NADH, feeding the electron transport chain in parallel to the canonical MPC/pyruvate route. The mitochondrial lactate individual is shared between the MCT1 'has output' and the LDHB 'has input' (handoff), so 'provides input for' is inferred and not materialised." ;
        lego:conforms_to_gpad  false ;
        lego:modelstate        "development" ;
        dc:contributor         "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7299-6685" ;
        dc:date                "2026-11-16" ;
        dc:title               "In heart, SLC16A1 directly transports lactate into mitochondria for conversion to pyruvate by LDHB, fueling the electron transport chain (Human)." ;
        pav:providedBy         "https://www.uniprot.org" , "http://geneontology.org" ;
        owl:versionIRI         gomodel:6ab067da00015739 ;
        biolink:in_taxon       obo:NCBITaxon_9606 .

